---

<Article
  title={title}
  description={description}
  heading={heading}
  subtitle={subtitle}
  updated={updated}
  schema={schema}
  breadcrumb={breadcrumb}
  cta={{ href: '/hearts/', label: 'Play Hearts' }}
>
  <p>
    Hearts is the classic trick-taking game of <em>avoidance</em>: unlike most card games, you are
    trying <strong>not</strong> to win the wrong tricks. Every heart costs you a point, the Queen of
    Spades costs thirteen, and the player with the <strong>lowest</strong> score when someone hits
    100 is the winner. This page is a complete reference to how Hearts is played. If you'd rather
    learn by doing, you can <a href="/hearts/">play Hearts</a> right now against three computer
    opponents.
  </p>

  <h2>What is Hearts?</h2>
  <p>
    Hearts is a four-player game played with a standard 52-card deck. There are no partnerships —
    it is every player for themselves. Aces are high and there is no trump suit. The goal is to dodge
    points across a series of hands, and the game ends when any player reaches the target score
    (100 on CardHearth). At that moment, the player with the <strong>fewest</strong> points wins.
  </p>

  <h2>The deal</h2>
  <p>
    All 52 cards are dealt out evenly, so each of the four players receives <strong>13 cards</strong>.
    Nothing is left in a stock — every card is in someone's hand. You sort your hand, and then,
    before a single card is played, comes the pass.
  </p>

  <h2>The pass</h2>
  <p>
    At the start of most hands you choose <strong>three cards</strong> to pass face down to another
    player, and you receive three from someone else. The direction <strong>rotates</strong> hand by
    hand:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Hand 1:</strong> pass left.</li>
    <li><strong>Hand 2:</strong> pass right.</li>
    <li><strong>Hand 3:</strong> pass across (to the player opposite you).</li>
    <li><strong>Hand 4:</strong> no pass — you keep your hand (the "hold" hand).</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Then the cycle repeats. You pick your three cards and pass them <em>before</em> you see what is
    coming back, so the pass is your first real decision: it is your chance to unload dangerous cards
    like the Queen of Spades, the Ace and King of Spades, or a long run of high hearts.
  </p>

  <h2>The two of clubs leads</h2>
  <p>
    The player holding the <strong>two of clubs</strong> must lead it to begin the first trick. Play
    then goes clockwise. Each player in turn plays one card, and you <strong>must follow the led
    suit</strong> if you hold a card in it. If you are void in the led suit, you may play any card —
    and that is exactly how you get rid of hearts and the Queen of Spades.
  </p>

  <h2>Winning a trick</h2>
  <p>
    The highest card of the <strong>led suit</strong> wins the trick — there is no trump, so off-suit
    cards (the ones you throw when you can't follow) can never win. Whoever wins the trick collects
    those four cards face down and leads the next trick. The cards you win don't help you; they are
    where your points come from.
  </p>

  <h2>The no-points-on-the-first-trick rule</h2>
  <p>
    You may <strong>not</strong> play any point card — no heart, and not the Queen of Spades — on the
    very first trick of a hand, unless your hand is somehow nothing but points. This keeps the
    opening trick safe and stops anyone dumping the Queen immediately.
  </p>

  <h2>Breaking hearts</h2>
  <p>
    You cannot <strong>lead</strong> a heart until hearts have been "broken". Hearts break the first
    time someone, unable to follow the led suit, discards a heart (or the Queen of Spades) onto a
    trick. Until that happens, hearts can only be played when you are following suit or sloughing on
    another suit — never as the card that opens a trick.
  </p>

  <h2>Scoring</h2>
  <p>At the end of each hand, players count the point cards they took:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>Each <strong>heart</strong> is worth <strong>1 point</strong> (thirteen hearts = 13 points).</li>
    <li>The <strong>Queen of Spades</strong> is worth <strong>13 points</strong> on her own.</li>
    <li>Every other card is worth nothing.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    So there are <strong>26 points</strong> in play every hand. Points are bad: you want to take as
    few as possible. Scores accumulate across hands, and the game ends when someone reaches
    <strong>100</strong>. The player with the lowest total then wins.
  </p>

  <h2>Shooting the moon</h2>
  <p>
    There is one spectacular exception. If a single player takes <strong>all 26 points</strong> in a
    hand — every heart and the Queen of Spades — they have <strong>shot the moon</strong>. Rather
    than scoring 26, that player scores <strong>zero</strong> and each of the other three players is
    hit with <strong>26 points</strong>. It is a high-risk, high-reward gambit: miss by a single
    heart and you simply collect all those points yourself.
  </p>

  <h2>How you win</h2>
  <p>
    Hands continue until a player's running total reaches 100 or more. The game then stops and the
    <strong>lowest</strong> score wins. Because a single bad hand — or one successful moon shot
    against you — can swing 26 points, Hearts stays tense right to the end.
  </p>

  <h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
  {
    faq.map((f) => (
      <details class="faq">
        <summary class="cursor-pointer font-semibold">{f.q}</summary>
        <p class="mt-2 opacity-90">{f.a}</p>
      </details>
    ))
  }

  <h2>Keep going</h2>
  <p>
    Now that you know the rules, <a href="/hearts/">play a hand of Hearts</a> against the computer.
    When you want to start dodging points like a pro, read our
    <a href="/guides/hearts-strategy/">Hearts strategy guide</a>, try the partnership game in
    <a href="/guides/spades-rules/">Spades</a>, or browse everything in our
    <a href="/guides/">guides hub</a>.
  </p>
</Article>
